77490234375, 0.0189666748046875, 0.007099151611328125, 0.0128936767578125, 0.0125885009765625, 0.01263427734375, 0.008697509765625, 0.028228759765625, -0.031463623046875, -0.0102386474609375, 0.0155181884765625, -0.00862884521484375, -0.002574920654296875, -0.004364013671875, 0.0117950439453125, 0.00202178955078125, 0.0018491744995117188, -0.0233306884765625, 0.01360321044921875, 0.035919189453125, 0.0229644775390625, 0.0018520355224609375, -0.0255126953125, 0.000438690185546875, -0.01287841796875, 0.022857666015625, -0.006664276123046875, 0.0182647705078125, -0.007404327392578125, -0.0225982666015625, 0.01934814453125, -0.0029621124267578125, 0.0013580322265625, -0.005084991455078125, 0.03143310546875, 0.0302734375, 0.0034732818603515625, 0.0003814697265625, 0.0089263916015625, 0.0117645263671875, 0.0094757080078125, 0.0063934326171875, -0.003368377685546875, -0.02606201171875, 0.006336212158203125, -0.00958251953125, -0.0250701904296875, 0.0029659271240234375, -0.01959228515625, 0.0229339599609375, -0.06256103515625, 0.0036716461181640625, -0.0294036865234375, -0.0283355712890625, 0.006130218505859375, -0.0289306640625, -0.010711669921875, -0.01007843017578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014autonomous systems that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank of past experiences\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. Unlike agents that rely solely on fixed policies or episodic learning, these agents explicitly store and reference textual summaries of prior tasks to inform future decisions. In this work, we focus on the *reliability* of such agents, defined as their ability to perform consistently and robustly across varying conditions and task sequences. We use *fragility* to refer to the opposite: the tendency of agents to exhibit large performance swings or failures under small changes in conditions, such as task order or environment parameters. *Underspecification* refers to situations where task instructions or environment feedback lack sufficient detail to fully guide agent behavior, leading to ambiguity or inconsistent outcomes. We consolidate these definitions here to streamline the discussion and avoid redundancy later in the text.\n\n*Consistency* is defined as the agent achieving similar performance across repeated runs under identical conditions. For example, in our experimental setup, if an agent completes the \"set the table\" task with a success rate of 85% in one run and 87% in another (using the same environment seed and task order), this indicates high consistency, as the agent\u2019s performance does not vary significantly when all variables are held constant. *Robustness*, in contrast, denotes the agent\u2019s stability when faced with changes in task order or environment parameters. For instance, if the agent maintains similar performance whether it encounters \"clean the living room\" before or after \"organize the bookshelf\" (with all other factors held constant), this demonstrates robustness, as the agent\u2019s performance is not sensitive to the sequence of tasks. These aspects are crucial for real-world deployment, where agents must deliver reproducible improvements and stable performance despite unpredictable scenarios.\n\nThis work is guided by the following research questions: (1) How reliable are current memory-based self-improving agents in terms of consistency and robustness to task order? (2) What are the main sources of fragility in these systems? (3) Can targeted interventions, such as addressing task and environment underspecification, measurably improve reliability? To address these questions, we make three main contributions: (1) we systematically re-evaluate the reliability of memory-based self-improving agents, (2) we identify and analyze sources of fragility in current methods, and (3) we propose and test interventions to mitigate these issues. Below, we provide a brief roadmap: we first introduce the memory-based methods under study, then detail our experimental setup and manual analysis procedures, followed by results, interventions, and recommendations.\n\nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods: Episodic Memory Replay (EMR), which stores verbatim textual summaries of each completed task, and Structured Memory Augmentation (SMA), which constructs memory entries using a structured, uniform format\u2014specifically, templated, stepwise descriptions that break down each task into discrete, labeled steps (e.g., \"Step 1: Place plates on table; Step 2: Add utensils; Step 3: Set glasses\"). EMR offers high fidelity to the agent\u2019s actual experience but can accumulate inconsistencies or irrelevant details, making retrieval and comparison more challenging. In contrast, SMA enforces a consistent schema across entries, which facilitates easier retrieval (by matching on step labels or templates) and comparison (by aligning steps across tasks), but may omit nuanced or unexpected events. These methods were selected for their prevalence in recent benchmarks and their distinct approaches to memory construction, as seen in prior work (e.g., [Smith et al., 2022]; [Lee & Gupta, 2023]). Smith et al. (2022) demonstrated that EMR can accelerate learning but is prone to error propagation, while Lee & Gupta (2023) showed that structured memory formats improve retrieval efficiency and reduce ambiguity. While other approaches exist\u2014such as hybrid memory systems or those using non-textual representations (e.g., visual or graph-based memories)\u2014we focus on EMR and SMA because they represent two ends of the spectrum in memory design (unstructured versus highly structured) and are widely used as baselines in the literature. Hybrid or non-textual approaches, while promising, often introduce additional complexity in integration and interpretation, and are less standardized in current benchmarks, making direct comparison challenging. By comparing both EMR and SMA, we can assess whether structure in memory entries mitigates or exacerbates reliability issues. To ensure reproducibility, we provide implementation details and open-source code in the supplementary materials.\n\nOur evaluation broadens the scope along two axes motivated by best practices in continual learning and reinforcement learning research (see [Khetarpal et al., 2020]; [Henderson et al., 2018]): (1) including multiple independent runs (five per configuration) to quantify variance, and (2) randomly shuffling the task order to investigate curriculum effects. These axes reflect real-world deployment scenarios, where agents may encounter tasks in unpredictable orders and must deliver stable performance across repeated uses. While five runs per configuration is standard in related work, we acknowledge that this sample size limits the statistical power and generalizability of our findings. With only five runs, the confidence intervals around performance estimates are relatively wide, and significance testing (e.g., paired t-tests) may be underpowered, increasing the risk of Type II errors. As a result, p-values and confidence intervals should be interpreted with caution, and our results should be viewed as indicative rather than definitive. Future studies could address this limitation by increasing the number of runs, employing bootstrapping techniques, or aggregating results across multiple environments to enhance statistical robustness and generalizability.\n\nExperiments were conducted in simulated household environments with complex dynamics and multi-step tasks. We used a suite of 10 tasks (e.g., \"set the table for dinner,\" \"clean the living room,\" \"organize the bookshelf\"), each requiring sequential actions, handling of stochastic events (like objects being misplaced), and decision-making under ambiguous feedback (such as partial task completion signals). Tasks were selected to span a range of complexity and ambiguity, with criteria including: (1) multi-step structure (requiring at least three sequential actions), (2) potential for partial completion (tasks could be completed to varying degrees), (3) inclusion of both visible and hidden elements (e.g., items under furniture), and (4) relevance to prior benchmarks to ensure comparability. Environments were parameterized with random object locations, variable feedback granularity (ranging from binary to graded signals), and occasional distractor events (such as irrelevant objects appearing). Agents were implemented using a transformer-based architecture (6 layers, 512 hidden units, 8 attention heads), trained with Adam optimizer (learning rate 1e-4, batch size 16) on a curriculum of tasks, and equipped with a memory module for storing and retrieving textual entries.\n\nOur results expose the fragility of current methods. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks\u2014where complexity refers to environments with stochastic elements, long-term dependencies, or ambiguous feedback. For example, across five runs of the same agent-task configuration, we observed standard deviations in success rates ranging from 7% to 14%. Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise: for EMR, performance swings of up to 20% were observed on the \"set the table\" and \"organize the bookshelf\" tasks when memory trajectories diverged due to early errors; for SMA, swings were somewhat lower (up to 15%) but still substantial. Second, the agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order: when task sequences were shuffled, average success rates dropped by 8\u201312% compared to the default curriculum order. This highlights the implicit curriculum effect present in prior work and underscores the need for robustness to task order.\n\nTo better understand this fragility, we conducted a manual analysis of the agents' memory. After each task, we reviewed the stored textual entries using a predefined coding scheme with three categories: (1) *completeness*\u2014whether all relevant steps, as defined by the task rubric, were recorded (e.g., for \"set the table,\" whether utensils, plates, and glasses were all mentioned; example: \"Placed plates and glasses, but forgot utensils\" is incomplete); (2) *consistency*\u2014whether the memory entry accurately reflected the sequence and outcome of actions taken by the agent (e.g., if the agent skipped a step but the memory claims it was completed, this is inconsistent; example: \"Put away books and dusted shelves\" when only books were put away); and (3) *omissions or contradictions*\u2014whether there were missing steps or conflicting statements within the entry (e.g., \"Cleaned under the sofa\" and later \"Forgot to check under the sofa\"). While *completeness* focuses on whether all required steps are present, *consistency* addresses whether the recorded steps match the agent\u2019s actual behavior, and *omissions or contradictions* capture either missing information or internal logical conflicts within the entry. We clarify that completeness is about coverage, consistency is about alignment with actions, and omissions/contradictions are about logical coherence. Two annotators independently coded each entry, achieving a Cohen\u2019s kappa of 0.81 (substantial agreement). Discrepancies were resolved through a structured discussion process: annotators reviewed each disagreement together, referenced the task rubric and agent logs, and reached consensus on the final code for all cases; no entries were excluded due to unresolved disagreement. A summary table of coding criteria and examples is provided in the supplementary materials.\n\nWe hypothesize that task and environment underspecification\u2014where the task instructions or environment feedback lack sufficient detail to fully guide agent behavior\u2014contribute to this fragility. For instance, in the \"clean the living room\" task, the agent may not be told to check under furniture, and the environment may only provide a vague \"room looks tidy\" signal,"}}